# CODEOWNERS for de-otio/trellis — task D3.
#
# Copied from dot-work/scripts/CODEOWNERS.template; only the repo-specific
# block at the bottom differs. A per-repo variation in the agent control plane
# is exactly the thing the daily posture check exists to notice.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS IS, TODAY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The `byo-full-public` ruleset REQUIRES one approving code-owner review, from
# someone other than the last pusher (the D5 "N=1" policy; see dot-work
# rulesets/README.md, "The N=1 policy"). This file is what gives that
# requirement something to bite on, and the daily assertion checks it exists.
# The org's one filled seat merges its own pull requests through the recorded
# `OrganizationAdmin` bypass (pull requests only), not through a bot owner.
# Consequence: a bot-authored pull request, Dependabot's included, has no
# approver by default, so its auto-merge waits until a founder approves it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THREE THINGS THAT MUST NOT CHANGE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 1. THE CATCH-ALL IS THE FIRST RULE. Later rules win in CODEOWNERS. A
#    catch-all placed anywhere else is overridden by everything above it, and an
#    absent catch-all means unlisted paths have no owner at all — which is how
#    a bot-authored pull request touching an unowned path passes a code-owner
#    requirement (finding S5).
#
# 2. NO `<slug>[bot]` ANYWHERE IN THIS FILE. A bot is a different GitHub
#    principal from its operator, so a bot code owner lets one human plus their
#    own agent satisfy review. There is no server-side prevention; the daily
#    check asserts the absence and D6d detects bot approvals after the fact.
#
# 3. THE OWNER MUST BE A HANDLE GITHUB RESOLVES. An unrecognised owner makes
#    the rule silently never match. `@de-otio/founders` (created 2026-09-12)
#    resolves in CODEOWNERS ONLY if the team has explicit write access to this
#    repository, even when every member already has it by another route.
#    Nothing asserts that the owner resolves; the grant is the control.

# --- catch-all: first rule, always ----------------------------------------
*                       @de-otio/founders

# --- agent control plane (finding S10) ------------------------------------
# Not documentation. A synced file that adds an MCP server or a
# postCreateCommand changes what every agent in the org may do. Also diffed by
# D6f against a founder-approved baseline.
/.mcp.json              @de-otio/founders
/.claude/               @de-otio/founders
/.devcontainer/         @de-otio/founders
/.vscode/               @de-otio/founders
/AGENTS.md              @de-otio/founders

# --- CI and the gate plane ------------------------------------------------
# `.github/**` owns the workflow files D6e asserts against. A change here is a
# change to what CI proves, which is a change to what a merge means.
/.github/               @de-otio/founders

